2. AI and Machine Learning Restrictions

The Software, including its source code, object code, documentation, examples,
tests, datasets, and other associated materials, must not be used, in whole or
in part, for the purpose of:

training, pre-training, fine-tuning, or otherwise developing an artificial
intelligence, machine learning, generative AI, foundation model, large
language model, multimodal model, or similar computational model;
creating, expanding, validating, benchmarking, or maintaining datasets
intended for training or improving such systems;
creating embeddings, vector databases, retrieval datasets, or other derived
representations for the purpose of training or improving such systems;
evaluating or testing an AI model where the Software or its contents are
incorporated into the model, training dataset, or other persistent model
resource; or
extracting, collecting, scraping, or otherwise systematically acquiring the
Software or its associated materials for any of the purposes described above.

This restriction applies regardless of whether the AI system is publicly
available, privately operated, commercial, non-commercial, open-source,
closed-source, local, hosted, or provided as a service.

3. Autonomous AI Use

Unless expressly authorised in writing by the copyright holder, the Software
must not be used, accessed, operated, controlled, or invoked by an autonomous
or semi-autonomous artificial intelligence agent for the purpose of performing
tasks on behalf of a user.

This includes, but is not limited to:

AI agents executing or controlling the Software;
autonomous AI systems interacting with the Software through an API;
AI systems creating accounts, submitting requests, or performing actions
through the Software;
using the Software as a tool, service, or component of an autonomous AI
agent; and
connecting the Software to an AI system in a manner that allows the AI
system to independently determine and perform actions using the Software.
4. AI-Assisted Human Development

The restrictions in Sections 2 and 3 do not prohibit an individual human
developer from using an AI-assisted development tool to:

understand the Software;
debug the Software;
explain source code;
generate or suggest modifications;
write documentation;
generate tests; or
otherwise assist the developer in personally developing or maintaining
software.

However, such use must not result in the Software or substantial portions of
its source code being used as training data, fine-tuning data, or other
persistent model-development data in violation of Section 2.

